The experiment uses a thioxanthone derivative as an external photosensitizer to drive light-dependent covalent labeling of lysate proteins by an alkyne-containing probe; the alkyne is subsequently detected by CuAAC with Cy5-azide. The first probe bears a phenolic substituent that readily forms a phenoxyl radical, producing strong light-dependent labeling. Replacing the phenol with a benzyl alcohol removes this pathway, yet a weaker but still observable light-dependent signal remains.  

The bicyclo[4.2.0]octadiene scaffold can undergo sensitized photo-retro-[2+2] opening to generate the electrophilic Michael acceptor methyl (E)-4-oxo-4-(prop-2-yn-1-ylamino)but-2-enoate. This species accounts for the residual, lower-intensity labeling seen with the second probe. None of the other options explain a light-dependent, alkyne-dependent signal once the phenolic radical pathway is eliminated.}

