14.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A. The thioxanthenone remains the only component that absorbs 417 nm and can therefore initiate any light-dependent covalent attachment of the alkyne handle once the phenolic OH is removed. With the hydroxymethyl probe the efficient phenoxyl-radical or PCET pathway is lost, yet the same sensitizer can still drive lower-yield H-atom abstraction or energy transfer directly from the bicyclo[4.2.0] core or benzylic CH2OH, producing the weaker but still observable light-versus-dark Cy5 difference after click.  

C cannot account for the graded attenuation: a retro-[2+2] opening to the Michael-acceptor enoate would be essentially independent of the remote phenyl substituent and should therefore yield comparable labeling in both probes, yet the data show a sharp drop when the phenol is replaced. In addition, no thermal or sensitized retro-[2+2] of this bicyclo system has been reported under these mild lysate conditions, and the strict requirement for both 417 nm illumination and the thioxanthenone presence is inconsistent with spontaneous fragmentation. B is eliminated by the structural change itself, D has no mechanistic basis in either probe, and E is added after irradiation.  

Thus only the unchanged thioxanthenone photosensitizer explains a light-triggered, alkyne-dependent signal that survives removal of the phenolic OH while displaying the observed reduction in efficiency.}

